      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To examine impact on perinatal outcome of untreated gestational diabetes (GDM) and non-diabetics stratified by body mass index (BMI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "RESEARCH DESIGN AND METHODS",
          "content": "This is a secondary analysis of our investigation of the consequences of not treating GDM. We evaluated 555 untreated GDMs matched to 1100 non-diabetics. BMI was determined using subjects' recalled pre-pregnancy weight. A primary composite variable consisted of stillbirth, neonatal macrosomia/large-for-gestational-age (LGA), neonatal hypoglycemia, erythrocytosis and hyperbilirubinemia. Secondary outcomes included shoulder dystocia, respiratory complications, cesarean delivery and pregnancy-related hypertension."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Untreated subjects in the normal weight category had an ∼2-fold increase for composite outcome and LGA and a 7-fold increase in metabolic complications. The overweight untreated group showed composite outcome, LGA and metabolic complications 2-3-fold higher and induction of labor 5-fold higher. For obese untreated GDMs, significantly higher rates of composite outcome, LGA and metabolic complications, induction of labor and cesarean delivery were 10-, 3-, 5-, 4- and 9-fold, respectively. Perinatal outcome for normal weight untreated GDM was similar to obese non-diabetics."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Maternal obesity and GDM independently affect adverse pregnancy outcome. The combination has a greater impact than each one alone. However, glycemic level contributes a greater portion to the adverse pregnancy equation."
        }
      ]},
